course: dictionary
lesson: 11522
title: Alleged: a claim, not yet proven
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you that something is a claim, not yet a fact. «Alleged» is one of them. When you read «the alleged thief», the writer is saying that someone accuses this person of stealing, but nobody has proved it yet.
explain: «The alleged thief ran past the bike shop with a red bag.» The clue is the word «thief»: someone has said the person took something, but a judge has not decided. So alleged means said to be true, but not yet proven.
explain: Reporters use «alleged» to be fair. A person is innocent until proven guilty, so the news writes «the alleged attack» while the court is still deciding. In an argument, if Sam says «Ali alleged that I cheated», Sam is claiming it without showing proof.
explain: Check the clues around the word. If you can swap in «said to be true» and the sentence still makes sense, you have got it. Alleged can also be a verb: «She alleged that the window was broken» means she claimed it, without proof.
fact: Alleged means said to be true, or said to have happened, but not yet proven.
fact: «The alleged thief» means someone has accused this person, but a court has not yet decided.
fact: «Allegedly» is the adverb: «He allegedly took the money» means people say he did, but it is not proven.
fact: The verb «allege» means to claim something is true without proof: «They alleged that the road was closed.»
fact: Reporters write «alleged» to be fair, because a person is innocent until proven guilty.
exercise: «The alleged thief was seen near the shop.» What does alleged mean here? | Happy and smiling | Proven to be true by a judge | Very quick and fast | *Said to be true, but not yet proven
exercise: Reporters write «the alleged attack» to be fair. Why? | It means the attack happened for certain | The attack was a game | *Because the attack is only a claim until a court decides | They want the attack to sound bigger
exercise: «Mia alleged that Tom broke the vase.» What did Mia do? | Proved that Tom broke it | Swept up the broken pieces | Dropped the vase herself | *Claimed Tom broke it, without proof
test: «The alleged robber wore a black hat.» What does alleged tell you? | The robber has been found guilty | He is definitely the robber | *People say he is the robber, but it is not proven | He is a famous hat maker
test: Which sentence uses alleged in the right way? | I ate an alleged apple for lunch | The alleged sun was shining on the sea | *It is alleged that the shop sold out-of-date milk, but the council is still looking into it | The alleged bus was very tall
exam: «It is alleged that the school bell rang early.» What is the best meaning? | It never rang at all | It rang early, and everyone saw it | It rang very loudly | *People say it rang early, but this is not yet proven
